1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,750
Somebody is being led up a mountain.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:16,690
Start at the bottom with them, at the edge of the lake, where the air is hot and
close and smells of water and reeds.

3
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:25,544
Then the path turns uphill and everything a blind person steers by begins to change.
The ground goes from sand to stone.

4
00:00:25,594 --> 00:00:32,371
The lake, which was beside you, is now below you, and its sound thins out and drops
away.

5
00:00:32,421 --> 00:00:43,330
There are no walls up here, no doorways, nothing for a voice to bounce off, so the
world goes wide and far and hard to read. Then the air.

6
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:52,336
It cools as you climb, and it starts to move. Down at the shore the wind came in
pieces, round a house, through a lane.

7
00:00:52,386 --> 00:01:03,079
Up here it comes all at once across open ground with nothing to slow it. Anyone who
cannot see knows what that means without being told. It means height.

8
00:01:03,129 --> 00:01:06,990
It means there is an edge somewhere, and no way of knowing where.

9
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:20,007
And then, from higher up, another sound comes down the hill. Not one voice.
Hundreds.

10
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:31,182
Shouting, and laughing, and somebody crying the way people cry when they cannot
believe what has just happened to them. It has been going on for three days.

11
00:01:31,232 --> 00:01:34,220
That sound is the reason for the climb.

12
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:44,354
Dozens are making it: the lame on stretchers, the crippled on backs, the mute led by
the hand, and the blind, who can walk perfectly well,

13
00:01:44,404 --> 00:01:49,935
holding on to an arm because the path is rock and they have never been here.

14
00:01:49,985 --> 00:01:56,013
At the top a man is sitting down, waiting for them. Nobody in this crowd gets a
name.

15
00:01:56,063 --> 00:02:02,710
This episode is about them, and about another crowd like them, in the Temple, a few
days before the end.

16
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:17,228
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

17
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:25,968
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

18
00:02:26,018 --> 00:02:28,910
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

19
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:43,898
First, where we are. Matthew chapter fifteen. Jesus has been away in the far north,
in the region of Tyre and Sidon, where He healed a Gentile woman's daughter.

20
00:02:43,948 --> 00:02:49,786
Now He comes back toward the Sea of Galilee, goes up a mountain, and sits down.

21
00:02:49,836 --> 00:03:00,819
Matthew Henry says He sat on a mountain so that all might see Him and have free
access to Him, and he calls Him an open Saviour.

22
00:03:00,869 --> 00:03:11,250
He sat there, Henry says, like a host waiting to welcome guests, waiting to be
gracious. Matthew chapter 15, verses 29 to 31.

23
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:20,491
[Reader] Moving on from there, Jesus went along the Sea of Galilee. Then He went up
on a mountain and sat down.

24
00:03:20,541 --> 00:03:31,351
[Reader] Large crowds came to Him, bringing the lame, the blind, the crippled, the
mute, and many others, and laid them at His feet, and He healed them.

25
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:42,370
[Reader] The crowd was amazed when they saw the mute speaking, the crippled
restored, the lame walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified the God of
Israel.

26
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:59,958
Three verses, and in them the lame, the blind, the crippled, the mute and many
others. Then Matthew tells us how long they had been up there, and what happened
next.

27
00:04:00,008 --> 00:04:03,610
Matthew chapter 15, verses 32 to 38.

28
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:16,059
[Reader] Then Jesus called His disciples to Him and said, “I have compassion for
this crowd, because they have already been with Me three days and have nothing to
eat.

29
00:04:16,109 --> 00:04:21,226
[Reader] I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may faint along the way.”

30
00:04:21,276 --> 00:04:32,087
[Reader] The disciples replied, “Where in this desolate place could we find enough
bread to feed such a large crowd?” “How many loaves do you have?” Jesus asked.

31
00:04:32,137 --> 00:04:39,691
[Reader] “Seven,” they replied, “and a few small fish.” And He instructed the crowd
to sit down on the ground.

32
00:04:39,741 --> 00:04:49,259
[Reader] Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He gave thanks and broke them. Then
He gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.

33
00:04:49,309 --> 00:04:57,473
[Reader] They all ate and were satisfied, and the disciples picked up seven
basketfuls of broken pieces that were left over.

34
00:04:57,523 --> 00:05:02,050
[Reader] A total of four thousand men were fed, besides women and children.

35
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:18,145
The place. Edersheim puts this scene on the eastern side of the lake, in the
Decapolis, the ten Greek cities, among people who were not strictly Jewish.

36
00:05:18,195 --> 00:05:26,256
That fits the last line of the healing, which says they glorified the God of Israel,
the way outsiders speak of someone else's God.

37
00:05:26,306 --> 00:05:35,832
Other commentators put it on the western shore. Matthew says only the Sea of Galilee
and a mountain. How high was it? Nobody knows.

38
00:05:35,882 --> 00:05:45,549
Matthew does not name the mountain and no one has identified it. What the old
dictionaries can tell us is what any climb from that shore was like.

39
00:05:45,599 --> 00:05:50,383
The surface of the lake lies nearly seven hundred feet below the level of the sea.

40
00:05:50,433 --> 00:06:01,397
Smith's dictionary says it is surrounded by a high and almost unbroken wall of
hills, and that the deep hollow makes the climate of the shore almost tropical,

41
00:06:01,447 --> 00:06:03,349
with intense heat in summer.

42
00:06:03,399 --> 00:06:16,059
So whichever hill this was, going up it meant climbing out of the heat into cooler,
moving air, with the lake falling away below. And Matthew adds one detail himself.

43
00:06:16,109 --> 00:06:27,789
The disciples call it a desolate place. There was no village at the top, and no
bread to be had. Three days. What were those days like?

44
00:06:27,839 --> 00:06:30,878
Not a feast, at least not until the end.

45
00:06:30,928 --> 00:06:42,147
Henry says the people went without their natural rest and lay like soldiers in the
field, and that they had scarcely enough food to keep life and soul together.

46
00:06:42,197 --> 00:06:49,195
Edersheim says whatever they had brought with them must have run out. But it was not
a grim camp either.

47
00:06:49,245 --> 00:06:54,261
Matthew says the crowd was amazed, and that they glorified God.

48
00:06:54,311 --> 00:07:04,548
Every time a lame man stood up, or a mute woman spoke, or a blind man looked around
him, several thousand people saw it happen.

49
00:07:04,598 --> 00:07:18,540
Hungry, sleeping on the ground, and loud with wonder: that is the mountain. Then, at
the end of it, He fed them all. Was it faith that got them up there?

50
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:31,234
Notice what the text leaves out. In our last episode Jesus asked two blind men, do
you believe I am able to do this? On the mountain He asks no one anything.

51
00:07:31,284 --> 00:07:32,866
Henry noticed it.

52
00:07:32,916 --> 00:07:44,248
The people brought their sick relations and friends along with them and laid them
down at His feet, and, he says, we read not of any thing they said to Him.

53
00:07:44,298 --> 00:07:53,238
Their condition spoke for them. No commentator on our shelf says in so many words
that faith carried them up the hill.

54
00:07:53,288 --> 00:08:03,530
But the climb is the only statement these people make, and they made it carrying
each other. Every blind person on that hillside had been led up it by someone.

55
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:15,826
Where this is. Take Edersheim's placing, on the hills east of the lake. Stand there
and face north, with north at twelve o'clock.

56
00:08:15,876 --> 00:08:24,095
Capernaum and Bethsaida, from our last two episodes, are about five hours' walk at
eleven o'clock, round the top of the lake.

57
00:08:24,145 --> 00:08:36,093
Behind you, down the Jordan valley, Jericho is about three days' walk at six
o'clock, and Jerusalem about three and a half days at seven o'clock. Hold on to
Jerusalem.

58
00:08:36,143 --> 00:08:41,370
The second half of this episode happens there.

59
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:55,399
Scripture gives us the crowd and the mountain and the three days. It does not give
us one pair of them. We imagine a blind man and the friend who got him there.

60
00:08:55,449 --> 00:08:57,650
This is a scene, not Scripture.

61
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:04,670
[Friend] Rock here, big one, step up. Good. Now it levels off for a while.

62
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:20,442
[Man] The air has changed. It is cooler, and the wind is coming from everywhere at
once. I cannot hear the lake beside me any more. I can hear it under me. How high
are we?

63
00:09:20,492 --> 00:09:21,310
[Man] Tell me the truth.

64
00:09:21,730 --> 00:09:27,280
[Friend] High enough that I am not looking down either. Keep hold of me. The path is
wide.

65
00:09:31,483 --> 00:09:34,720
[Man] Wait. Listen. What is that, up ahead?

66
00:09:35,770 --> 00:09:43,240
[Friend] That is them. That is the crowd. Somebody up there has just started
shouting, and now the rest of them have joined in.

67
00:09:43,290 --> 00:09:47,080
[Man] They sound glad. How many are up there? It sounds like a market on a feast
day.

68
00:09:47,630 --> 00:09:57,580
[Friend] Thousands. More coming up behind us. There are men carrying a boy on a
door. A woman with a child who does not talk. Everybody.

69
00:09:58,255 --> 00:09:59,760
[Man] And him?

70
00:09:59,810 --> 00:10:11,360
[Friend] Sitting on a rock at the top, like he has all day. People go up to him one
at a time and then they, I do not know how to say it. They stand up different.

71
00:10:08,210 --> 00:10:19,829
[Man] Thyme crushed under everyone's feet. Smoke from somebody's fire. We have been
walking since before dawn and my friend has not let go of my arm once.

72
00:10:19,879 --> 00:10:22,640
[Man] I was nervous at the bottom. I am not now.

73
00:10:24,690 --> 00:10:30,400
[Friend] Nearly there. I am going to sit you down right in front of him. Then I will
step back.

74
00:10:31,950 --> 00:10:33,020
[Man] Do not go far.

75
00:10:33,070 --> 00:10:37,980
[Friend] I am not going anywhere. I did not walk you up a mountain to lose you at
the top.

76
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:45,190
Matthew says they laid them at His feet, and He healed them.

77
00:10:49,326 --> 00:11:02,058
And now, a word from Brian. Or rather, from the voice Brian picked for himself,
since he would not use his own. Around here we call it Mister Scaredy Cat.

78
00:11:02,108 --> 00:11:05,590
The voice is not his, but the words are.

79
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:08,344
[Brian] Up a mountain.

80
00:11:08,394 --> 00:11:17,224
[Brian] That sounds awfully scary to most blind people not named Lonnie Bedwell, who
is a blind Navy veteran and climbs the kind of mountains the rest of us read about.

81
00:11:17,274 --> 00:11:26,711
[Brian] I can tell when I have come to a place that is not usual. Up high it would
feel very different. The air is cooler. The wind blows with nothing to slow it down.

82
00:11:26,761 --> 00:11:30,542
[Brian] The sounds change, because there is nothing around you for them to bounce
off.

83
00:11:30,592 --> 00:11:36,662
[Brian] And I would get more nervous the higher we went, because I would know there
was an edge somewhere and not know where.

84
00:11:36,712 --> 00:11:39,084
[Brian] There is another thing sighted people may not think of.

85
00:11:39,134 --> 00:11:45,658
[Brian] I have lost a lot of endurance and stamina since I went blind, because I do
not get to do the physical things I used to do.

86
00:11:45,708 --> 00:11:52,191
[Brian] A long walk on flat ground is harder for me than it was. An uneven, unpaved
road is harder still.

87
00:11:52,241 --> 00:12:00,699
[Brian] So walking up a mountain over rough ground would have been very difficult
for a blind person, and that is before you remember that nobody thought to bring
food.

88
00:12:00,749 --> 00:12:09,134
[Brian] And yet if I had been given my sight up there, I sure would have hung
around. I would not have missed a moment of it. It would have been a sight to
behold.

89
00:12:09,184 --> 00:12:18,998
[Brian] So think about what it took. A blind person going up that mountain is
trusting two things at once. He is trusting the person on his arm, every single
step.

90
00:12:19,048 --> 00:12:25,987
[Brian] And he is trusting that at the top there is someone worth the climb. I think
what would have carried me up is the sound.

91
00:12:26,037 --> 00:12:30,499
[Brian] If people were being healed up there for three days, it was not quiet.

92
00:12:30,549 --> 00:12:38,607
[Brian] I would have heard shouting and laughing and crying coming down the hill
toward me, and that would have done more for my nerves than anything my guide could
say.

93
00:12:38,657 --> 00:12:48,633
[Brian] I wrote a song recently, and one of the lines goes, I will sing before I
see. It is about trusting Jesus, and the people on that mountain were doing just
that.

94
00:12:48,683 --> 00:12:57,380
[Brian] They were singing and praising without knowing where their next meal was
coming from, and He fed them afterward. And I think of the joy of the companions.

95
00:12:57,430 --> 00:13:00,540
[Brian] The people who help us are carrying our burdens too.

96
00:13:00,590 --> 00:13:07,037
[Brian] They made that climb with somebody on their arm or on their back, and they
went hungry for three days alongside us.

97
00:13:07,087 --> 00:13:14,951
[Brian] When their friend stood up healed, they must have felt great joy too. Nobody
on that mountain has a name. That is most of us.

98
00:13:15,001 --> 00:13:18,475
[Brian] Most blind people in history did not get a verse of their own.

99
00:13:18,525 --> 00:13:28,695
[Brian] They got carried up a hill by a brother or a daughter or a neighbor who
decided that day was the day, and they were laid down at somebody's feet, and that
was that.

100
00:13:28,745 --> 00:13:34,930
[Brian] I think about the carriers as much as the carried. Every one of those people
had someone who said, I will get you there.

101
00:13:38,703 --> 00:13:46,138
[Brian] I keep asking myself whether this is about needing help in order to get our
attention on Jesus. Every one of those people had to be brought.

102
00:13:46,188 --> 00:13:48,739
[Brian] Not one of them could have done it alone.

103
00:13:48,789 --> 00:13:56,848
[Brian] I did not choose blindness, but I know that needing an arm has pointed me
toward Him more times than being able to manage on my own ever did.

104
00:13:56,898 --> 00:14:03,650
[Brian] I do not say that is why any of us is blind. I only say it is what happened
on that mountain, and it is what happens to me.

105
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:18,130
Now the second crowd, and to understand it we have to go back a thousand years, to
the day David took Jerusalem. 2 Samuel chapter 5, verses 6 to 8.

106
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:31,179
[Reader] Now the king and his men marched to Jerusalem against the Jebusites who
inhabited the land. The Jebusites said to David: “You will never get in here.

107
00:14:31,229 --> 00:14:44,443
[Reader] Even the blind and lame can repel you.” For they thought, “David cannot get
in here.” Nevertheless, David captured the fortress of Zion (that is, the City of
David).

108
00:14:44,493 --> 00:14:54,341
[Reader] On that day he said, “Whoever attacks the Jebusites must use the water
shaft to reach the lame and blind who are despised by David.”

109
00:14:54,391 --> 00:14:59,970
[Reader] That is why it is said, “The blind and the lame will never enter the
palace.”

110
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:12,908
The Jebusites taunted David: even the blind and the lame could keep you out. David
took the city anyway, and a saying grew up from the insult.

111
00:15:12,958 --> 00:15:17,247
The blind and the lame will never enter the palace.

112
00:15:17,297 --> 00:15:24,638
Henry notes that the blind and the lame in the Jebusites' taunt may even have meant
their idols,

113
00:15:24,688 --> 00:15:31,799
and he is the one who reads the saying as a bar on blind and lame people entering
the Temple.

114
00:15:31,849 --> 00:15:37,367
Our shelf has no rabbinic rule that says so, and we found none.

115
00:15:37,417 --> 00:15:49,333
What is certain is that the saying was remembered, and that Matthew, who knew his
Scriptures, chose to tell us exactly who came into the Temple on this day.

116
00:15:49,383 --> 00:16:03,710
Matthew chapter twenty-one. Jesus has ridden into Jerusalem on a donkey to shouts of
Hosanna. He goes straight to the Temple. Matthew chapter 21, verses 12 to 16.

117
00:16:05,399 --> 00:16:11,632
[Reader] Then Jesus entered the temple courts and drove out all who were buying and
selling there.

118
00:16:11,682 --> 00:16:16,999
[Reader] He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those
selling doves.

119
00:16:17,049 --> 00:16:27,155
[Reader] And He declared to them, “It is written: ‘My house will be called a house
of prayer.’ But you are making it ‘a den of robbers.’”

120
00:16:27,205 --> 00:16:31,248
[Reader] The blind and the lame came to Him at the temple, and He healed them.

121
00:16:31,298 --> 00:16:41,984
[Reader] But the chief priests and scribes were indignant when they saw the wonders
He performed and the children shouting in the temple courts, “Hosanna to the Son of
David!”

122
00:16:42,034 --> 00:16:47,799
[Reader] “Do You hear what these children are saying?” they asked. “Yes,” Jesus
answered.

123
00:16:47,849 --> 00:16:53,530
[Reader] “Have you never read: ‘From the mouths of children and infants You have
ordained praise’?”

124
00:16:56,080 --> 00:17:04,677
Where this is. We are back in the city where this series began, three and a half
days' walk south of that mountain.

125
00:17:04,727 --> 00:17:08,661
The Temple Mount stands on the east side of Jerusalem.

126
00:17:08,711 --> 00:17:17,335
The Pool of Siloam, where the man born blind washed in our first episode, is a short
walk downhill from it, at the south end of the city.

127
00:17:17,385 --> 00:17:29,216
And the road Bartimaeus's crowd climbed in our second episode comes in from Jericho,
about six hours' walk away at two o'clock. And notice when this is.

128
00:17:29,266 --> 00:17:34,769
Bartimaeus was healed on that road, on the way up to this Passover.

129
00:17:34,819 --> 00:17:42,582
Now, within a day or so of Jesus riding into the city, the blind come to Him in the
Temple and He heals them.

130
00:17:42,632 --> 00:17:55,349
Two healings of blind people in His last week, with the cross a few days off. In
Matthew's Gospel this one in the Temple is the last healing of all. The place.

131
00:17:55,399 --> 00:18:00,708
The outer court of Herod's Temple, the Court of the Gentiles, worked as a market.

132
00:18:00,758 --> 00:18:08,870
Edersheim describes the official money changers who, for a fee, turned foreign coins
into the Temple's own coin,

133
00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:13,097
and what he calls that great mart for sacrificial animals.

134
00:18:13,147 --> 00:18:22,117
Herod had doubled the size of the Temple Mount to about fourteen hectares, and the
long porches around the edge were where the business was done.

135
00:18:22,167 --> 00:18:29,403
Into that noise of coins and cattle Jesus walks and turns the tables over.

136
00:18:29,453 --> 00:18:37,711
As the traders are driven out, Edersheim pictures the blind and the lame coming in
from the porches and the Temple Mount to be healed,

137
00:18:37,761 --> 00:18:41,555
and the children taking up the shout of Hosanna from the road.

138
00:18:41,605 --> 00:18:51,393
He guesses the children may have been sons of the Levites who sang in the Temple
choir, which would explain why they were there and why they knew what to sing.

139
00:18:51,443 --> 00:19:02,411
Matthew Henry set the scene against David's saying. The blind and the lame had been
shut out of David's palace, and here they were received in God's house.

140
00:19:02,461 --> 00:19:09,523
The Temple, he said, was profaned when it was made a market and honoured when it was
made a hospital.

141
00:19:09,573 --> 00:19:21,055
And he answers the crowd's question from the day before, who is this, with the
healings: Christ's works, he says, testified more than the hosannas.

142
00:19:21,105 --> 00:19:31,285
One more thing from the Law. A blind descendant of Aaron could not serve at the
altar, Leviticus says, though he was still fed from the holy offerings.

143
00:19:31,335 --> 00:19:43,228
So blind men had always been in the Temple, as priests' sons who could not serve and
as beggars at the gates. What was new on this day was not that they were present.

144
00:19:43,278 --> 00:19:45,310
It was that they were the point.

145
00:19:48,360 --> 00:19:52,154
[Brian] The blind and the lame will never enter the palace.

146
00:19:52,204 --> 00:19:59,966
[Brian] For a thousand years that saying hung around Jerusalem, and whether or not
it was ever an actual rule at the Temple, people repeated it.

147
00:20:00,016 --> 00:20:09,380
[Brian] Then Jesus clears the market out of the Temple courts, and the first people
through the gap are the blind and the lame. I do not think that is an accident of
timing.

148
00:20:09,430 --> 00:20:17,194
[Brian] I think He made room and they knew it. The leaders were angry about the
children shouting. Matthew puts the healing and the anger in the same breath.

149
00:20:17,244 --> 00:20:23,521
[Brian] The blind could see, the lame could walk, kids were singing, and the men in
charge were indignant.

150
00:20:23,571 --> 00:20:31,830
[Brian] When something good happens to people who are usually kept at the edges,
somebody in charge is usually annoyed. That has not changed much in two thousand
years.

151
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:49,349
Set the two crowds side by side. On the mountain, in Gentile country, people laid
their blind at His feet and glorified the God of Israel.

152
00:20:49,399 --> 00:21:00,131
In the Temple, the holiest ground in the Jewish world, the blind came to Him through
the space where the market had been, and the men in charge were indignant.

153
00:21:00,181 --> 00:21:10,784
Different ground, different crowd, same thing in the middle: blind people, brought
or coming, and Jesus healing them in front of everyone.

154
00:21:10,834 --> 00:21:18,090
Matthew wants us to see that it happened everywhere He went, and that the only
people who minded were the ones who had been keeping the gate.

155
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:29,336
[Brian] Here is what I take from these two crowds. The mountain was Gentile country,
and the people glorified the God of Israel.

156
00:21:29,386 --> 00:21:38,049
[Brian] The Temple was the holiest place in the Jewish world, and the people who had
been shut out came in. Blind people were in the middle of both.

157
00:21:38,099 --> 00:21:43,050
[Brian] We were never just simply the edge of what Jesus was doing. We ... were the
evidence.

158
00:21:45,823 --> 00:21:54,292
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at blindness
in the Bible dot com.

159
00:21:54,342 --> 00:22:05,340
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

160
